docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:217:| `sd(id) ~ x_group` | Implemented in scoped routes | Gaussian ordinary `mu` random intercepts and one independent numeric slope selected with `sd(id, coef = "x") ~ w` in native TMB; the paired Julia development bridge has one source-bound q1 slope parity fixture. Native ordinary Bernoulli/binomial `mu` intercepts; native Poisson ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope; complete-data native zero-inflated Poisson `mu` intercepts and one selected independent numeric slope with fixed-effect `zi`; native Gamma ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope; and native complete-data NB2 and zero-truncated NB2 ordinary `mu` intercepts plus Tweedie ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope; native complete-data Beta ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope, with the shared Beta slope slice checked at fixed `sigma ~ 1`; and native complete-data beta-binomial ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope, cumulative-logit ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope, zero-one beta, native lognormal, Student-t, and skew-normal ordinary `mu` intercepts, with independent numeric slopes for native lognormal and skew-normal. Native TMB and Julia also support complete-data zero-one beta `sd(group) ~ predictors` for one ordinary `mu` intercept or one selected independent numeric slope with fixed-intercept `sigma`, `zoi`, and `coi`; structured effects remain unsupported, and interval calibration is open. Julia Bernoulli/binomial SD regression exists only in the DRM.jl development candidate and was tested with explicitly matched local drmTMB and DRM.jl source checkouts; current shared/released DRM.jl does not include these routes. Candidate checks cover separate coefficient-selected SD formulas for independent scalar Bernoulli terms sharing a group, one ordinary correlated q2 intercept-slope block with shared constant correlation, and one relmat q1 intercept with a matching `sd(id, level = "relmat") ~ z` formula. The relmat route has one same-data point-parity fit against native TMB; labelled K/A matrices are aligned by group first appearance, while unlabelled matrices are positional; it does not establish interval calibration. An omitted member in the ordinary correlated block keeps a constant SD. Live R-to-Julia bridge checks in matched candidate checkouts cover fitting, recovery, extraction, prediction, save/reload, profile routing, and three bootstrap refits. A fit emitted a near-singular-Hessian warning; these checks do not establish same-data q2 parity or interval calibration. Other Julia SD routes mentioned in earlier development records likewise require matching candidate source and are not claims about the current shared/released DRM.jl. The candidate Julia Poisson slope route has recovery, prediction, an independent quadrature check, and one same-data comparison with native TMB; candidate Gamma recovery tests also pass. These narrow routes lack interval calibration. The zero-inflated Poisson route keeps `zi` fixed-effect only. Cumulative-logit direct-SD regression requires complete responses and admits one ordinary `mu` intercept or one independent numeric slope selected with `coef`; zero-truncated NB2 requires complete responses and one ordinary `mu` intercept in its direct-SD slice; zero-inflated Poisson requires complete responses and admits one `mu` intercept or one selected independent numeric slope in its direct-SD slice. Hurdle NB2 requires complete responses, fixed-effect `sigma` and `hu`, and one ordinary `mu` intercept or selected independent numeric slope for the paired TMB/Julia direct-SD slice. Zero-one beta requires complete responses and supports one ordinary `mu` intercept or one selected independent numeric `mu` slope in native TMB and Julia. These are separate family/backend slices, not support for every family or random-effect type. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:241:| `count ~ x + provider(1 + x | p | group, input = object)`, `family = poisson(link = "log")` | Exact labelled q2 point-fit slice | Ordinary non-zero-inflated Poisson admits exactly one labelled intercept-plus-one-numeric-slope `mu` block for `phylo(tree = tree)`, `spatial(coords = coords)`, `animal(Ainv = Ainv)`, or `relmat(Q = Q)`. It estimates two latent SDs and their intercept--slope correlation, but its three direct targets are visible and not profile-ready; retained local recovery evidence is point-fit-only. Pure structured slopes, multiple structured slopes, q4+, zero-inflated structure, simultaneous providers, ordinary count random effects, alternate provider inputs, intervals, and coverage remain outside this slice. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:1197:| `mu`, `mu1`, `mu2` | Yes for univariate Gaussian `mu`; random intercepts, independent numeric random slopes, and labelled or unlabelled ordinary correlated intercept-slope blocks are implemented. For non-zero-inflated Poisson and NB2 models, ordinary unlabelled `mu` random intercepts and independent numeric slopes such as `(1 | id) + (0 + x | id)` are implemented on the log-mean scale, one q=1 structured `mu` intercept may use `phylo()`, `phylo_interaction()`, `spatial()`, `animal()`, or `relmat()`, and one unlabelled intercept-plus-one-slope term may use `phylo()`, `spatial()`, `animal()`, or `relmat()`. Ordinary Poisson additionally admits unique labelled scalar ordinary intercepts on the complete-data route, including `sd(id, block = "p") ~ w`; it also admits one complete-data unlabelled `(1 + x | id)` ML-Laplace route at `point_fit_recovery` (`mc-0718`) and the exact labelled q2 intercept--slope forms for `phylo()`, `spatial()`, `animal()`, and `relmat()`; ordinary NB2 admits the matching unlabelled ordinary `(1 + x | id)` route at `point_fit_recovery` (`mc-0719`); ordinary lognormal admits the matching unlabelled ordinary `(1 + x | id)` route at `point_fit_recovery` (`mc-0720`) and the exact phylo labelled q2 form only. These count-q2 targets are point-fit-only and not profile-ready. Row-specific zero-inflated gates also accept an intercept-only spatial `mu` field for fixed-`zi` Poisson and fixed-`zi` NB2 at diagnostic-only grade. Ordinary Student-t, zero-truncated NB2, lognormal, Gamma, beta, beta-binomial, and binomial models also support unlabelled `mu` random intercepts and independent numeric slopes such as `(1 | id) + (0 + x | id)`. Binomial additionally admits one experimental complete-data unlabelled `(1 + x | id)` point-fit route. Beta additionally admits exactly one unlabelled intercept-only `phylo(1 | species, tree = tree)` term in `mu` at `point_fit_recovery` for the exact tested `g = 1024, m = 4` regime. The separate q1 direct-SD exception `sd(species, level = "phylogenetic") ~ w` has exact-regime profile-coverage evidence with the caveats recorded for `mc-0017`. Family `sigma` random effects are limited to listed routes; Gamma additionally supports one complete-data ordinary `sigma` intercept with `sd(group, dpar = "sigma") ~ predictors` and fixed-effect `mu`. This models variation in the latent log-CV effect; native recovery and constant-SD equivalence pass, while interval coverage and Julia parity remain unverified. Other family `sigma` combinations remain fixed-effect-only or separately scoped; phylogenetic random-effect slopes, labels/q2+, broader designs and broader interval/coverage claims remain deferred. Correlated non-Gaussian slopes outside the exact binomial, ordinary Poisson, and ordinary NB2 q2 point-fit routes, covariance labels outside the exact count-q2 forms, pure or multiple structured count slopes, zero-inflated structured count effects outside exact local-fit gates, bounded-response exact-boundary random effects, and richer or labelled NB2 structured `sigma` effects beyond the exact q1 recovery-grade routes remain planned. For bivariate Gaussian models, matching labelled random intercepts in `mu1` and `mu2`, such as `(1 | p | id)` in both formulas, matching slope-only `mu1`/`mu2` blocks such as `(0 + x | p | id)`, matching same-response slope-only `mu1`/`sigma1` or `mu2`/`sigma2` blocks, matching one-slope q=4 location blocks such as `(1 + x | p | id)`, matching q=6 location blocks such as `(1 + x + z | p | id)`, and the first all-four q8 endpoint block with matching `(1 + x | p | id)` terms in `mu1`, `mu2`, `sigma1`, and `sigma2` are implemented. Bivariate residual-scale slope-only `sigma1`/`sigma2` blocks are listed under `sigma`; formal simulation recovery for q > 2 bivariate location and q8 blocks is later. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:1199:| `sd(group)` | Scoped direct-SD routes are implemented for one or more distinct unlabelled univariate Gaussian `mu` intercepts; selected native complete-data ordinary `mu` intercepts in Bernoulli/binomial, Poisson, NB2, Gamma, Tweedie, Beta, beta-binomial, lognormal, Student-t, and skew-normal; and one Poisson slope-only target using `sd(id, coef = "x") ~ w` with `(0 + x | id)`. Bernoulli, Poisson, Gamma, ordinary NB2, Tweedie, Beta, beta-binomial, and cumulative-logit have native-TMB ordinary-intercept and independent-slope routes. Gamma and Beta additionally have one native q1 animal intercept SD route with pedigree, A, or Ainv input; Gamma also has a separate q1 animal slope-only coefficient route with group-level SD predictors and one q2 animal intercept-plus-independent-slope route with a separate `coef`-selected SD formula for each field. The combined Gamma q2 recovery/workflow fixture currently uses pedigree input; the separate q1 Gamma slope-only A/Ainv provider rows remain unverified. Complete-data NB2 has a separate q1 animal route for one intercept or one slope-only coefficient with group-level SD predictors; it does not admit a multi-coefficient block. Beta-binomial has the same three animal matrix inputs for its q1 `mu` intercept route. Beta's focused tests check point recovery, prediction, constant-SD equivalence, and supplied-matrix likelihood agreement with the pedigree fit. Beta-binomial's A/Ainv tests check point recovery, group predictions, and likelihood agreement with pedigree under a joint matrix-label permutation. Julia parity and interval calibration remain open. Julia Bernoulli/binomial SD regression, including separate coefficient-selected SD formulas and one correlated q2 block, exists only in the DRM.jl development candidate and was tested with explicitly matched local drmTMB and DRM.jl source checkouts; current shared/released DRM.jl does not include it. Candidate bridge checks cover q2 fitting, recovery, extraction, group-SD prediction, save/reload, profile routing, and three bootstrap refits. A fit emitted a near-singular-Hessian warning; this is not same-data q2 parity or interval calibration. Other Julia SD routes noted here are likewise candidate-only and require matching candidate source. The candidate Julia Poisson slope route has an independent quadrature check and one same-data engine comparison; the candidate Gamma animal route has one same-data non-unit-diagonal `A` comparison of fitted SD coefficients, log likelihood, and named group SDs. These are narrow recovery checks, not calibrated uncertainty evidence. Predictors must be constant within group after missing-row filtering. The Beta q1 phylogenetic exception `sd(spp_id, level = "phylogenetic") ~ x_tau` targets latent `tau`, not family `sigma`; ordinary and phylogenetic Beta direct-SD formulae cannot yet be combined. Explicit selectors resolve target identity; multiple coefficient-specific SD formulas on one group are implemented for separate independent Bernoulli terms, one ordinary correlated Bernoulli/binomial q = 2 block, the native ordinary Poisson q = 2 block, and the native Gamma animal q2 route just described. Correlated-block SD regression for other families or providers, and interval calibration, remain open. Gamma ordinary intercept and slope, Gamma animal slope-only, and Tweedie intercept and slope each have one synthetic recovery fit; Gamma intercept and ordinary slope and Tweedie intercept and slope also have constant-SD equivalence checks. Beta has one higher-information recovery fit and constant-SD equivalence check; beta-binomial has an ordinary-intercept recovery/oracle fixture and a separate independent-slope recovery, prediction, simulation, and save/reload smoke check; Student-t and skew-normal each have one recovery fit. Interval calibration, wider random-effect shapes, and remaining family routes are open. Gamma also admits one complete-data ordinary `sigma` random intercept with `sd(group, dpar = "sigma") ~ predictors` and fixed-effect `mu`; this targets the SD of the latent log-CV effect. Native recovery, conditional output, save/reload, within-group validation, and constant-SD equivalence checks pass; interval coverage and Julia parity remain open. |
